[{:seon.task/name "a-private-wait-wakes-for-the-fiber-dump"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-26: kill -INFO on a hung Seon test runner (pid 71239) printed no fiber dump. Its main thread was in a plain thread's read, which jolt.io-poller blocks in a private kqueue of its own (wait-ready on a plain thread: a private kevent); that kqueue has no SIGINFO filter, so the dump's Chez handler never got an event check. The dump (jolt.host/fiber-dump, Jolt d598fc04, docs/forks.md) wakes only a main carrier idle in the poller's own kqueue, which carries EVFILT_SIGNAL for SIGINFO; a test runner, the commonest hang, waits in the private one. Correct, at the cause: the private per-wait kqueue also takes SIGINFO (EVFILT_SIGNAL), so the wait wakes, its thread runs the handler at its event check, and the wait resumes (a signal is no readiness: the loop goes back into kevent). Proof red first: a plain thread blocked reading a pipe nobody writes, kill -INFO to its own process, and the dump's text on stderr while the read still waits (a Jolt unit or smoke case)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fiber-waits-for-a-childs-exit"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the blocking-calls audit of 2026-10-25 (now in docs/failure-points.md; this task holds its open entries). What exists: no fiber-aware wait for a child exit in jolt.io-poller. Process.waitFor is a WNOHANG poll with backoff (host/chez/java/process.ss:2230-2240), seon.lib/wait-status (lib.clj, the test child after its pipe EOF) is a blocking waitpid, and the fork server waits on its own kqueue with seon.lib/kq-wait (e13_wait, :blocking, fork_server.clj:185), which holds a main carrier until root writes or a child exits. Correct, in the poller (a Jolt branch jolt/<topic>): wait-ready-style park on a pid, macOS EVFILT_PROC with NOTE_EXIT on the poller kqueue, Linux pidfd_open (kernel 5.3) readable on exit; Process.waitFor and seon.lib/wait-status then park on it. Replaces the waitFor poll; enables seon.fork-server/the-fork-servers-idle-wait-is-two-parks. Proof to write: a fiber waiting on a child that exits later returns its status while a second fiber keeps running (no carrier held)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-io-poller-wakes-on-signals-on-linux"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the blocking-calls audit of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md) and the guard audit of the same day (docs/failure-points.md, J15). What exists: jolt.io-poller/wake-on-signals! is kqueue EVFILT_SIGNAL and answers false off macOS (stdlib/jolt/io_poller.clj:735), so seon.repl/run! (repl.clj:573) runs an agent on the main carrier on macOS only and on plain threads elsewhere; the same set of constants in seon.sock and seon.lib (SOL_SOCKET, MSG_DONTWAIT, FD_CLOFORK, SIGUSR1 30, SIGIO 23) is macOS-only. A silent false is a mode that differs by OS. Correct: Linux wakes through signalfd (or a self-pipe) in the poller, else the call is an error on Linux, never false. A signal that lands just before epoll_wait must not be lost. Proof to write: a signal sent to an idle main-carrier process wakes its loop (the test seon.intr already uses on macOS) under the Linux poller. Prerequisite of seon.repl/an-agent-runs-in-one-mode."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-thread-in-a-forked-child-collects-without-end"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-28 (root: ./seon forks line-parks at ~35 GB RSS, orphaned 8 h, and again as pid 89726). The line-parks fork case started a future (Thread/sleep 200, then a write) in a child forked from a main carrier, whose own fiber then parked in jolt.io-poller/wait-ready on a line-reader. Sampled (the 2026-10-28 evidence (working papers, not kept)): the future's thread in S_do_gc for the whole sample, sweep_continuation and copy_stack copying ever larger continuations, RSS 3.9 GB after 4 s at 99% CPU; the main thread in s_mutex_acquire, waiting on the collector; the run ended after 97-113 s when it ended at all, on main's binary as on this branch. Not the reader: the main thread does not spin. The test no longer starts a thread in the child (its writer is the parent's thread), so this is open in Jolt only. Cause to find: what the future's thread allocates without bound in a forked child (a sleep or a timer that captures continuations on a thread that is not a carrier?). Proof to write: a Jolt gate that forks from a main carrier, starts a future with a sleep in the child while its main fiber parks on a socket, and asserts the child ends with bounded allocation (bytes, not time)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "wake-on-signals-on-linux"
  :seon.task/plan "jolt.io-poller/wake-on-signals! wakes a main carrier's loop on a signal through kqueue's EVFILT_SIGNAL and answers false on Linux (its docstring: Linux would need a signalfd), so Seon ran its agent on the main carrier only on macOS (seon.repl/run!'s lib/macos? gate, removed by seon.agent/the-agent-is-a-flow-graph). Correct: on Linux the poller's epoll set holds a signalfd for the signals asked, so the loop wakes on them as on macOS. Its gates in an arm64 Linux container (Docker)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.io-poller-test/a-main-carrier-wakes-on-a-signal}}]
